  How often the database says it read each table (pg_stat_all_tables delta)
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Over 20 page loads, not one, and the per-load figure is
  the division. Two things make a one-request delta here worth less than it looks,
  and between them they are how T-139 read three statements as nine scans:

    · A backend flushes its counters when a transaction ends and at most about
      once a second, and it flushes when it is next used. So a snapshot taken
      after a burst of page loads can still have several of them in flight, and
      they land in the next window and read as that request's work. Spreading the
      window over 20 loads makes that a rounding error instead of a multiplier.
    · A scan is a plan execution, not a statement: one query driving a nested loop
      over 26 rows registers 26 index scans. So this table answers 'how much work
      did the plans do', and the statement counts above answer 'how many times did
      the application ask'. T-140 is about the second question.

  table                   seq scans  idx scans       seq rows       idx rows idx scans/load
